# ABSTRACT

Exploring alternative biomedical use of traditional drugs in different disease models is highly important as it can reduce the cost of drug development and overcome several critical issues of traditional chemodrugs such as low chemotherapeutic efficiency, severe side effect, and drug resistance. Disulfiram (DSF), a clinically approved alcohol-aversion drug, was recently demonstrated to feature tumor-growth suppression effect along with the co-administration of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) species, but direct \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) administration mode might cause severe toxicity originating from low \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) accumulation into the tumor and nonspecific \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) distribution-induced cytotoxicity. Based on the intriguing drug-delivery performance of nanoscale metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), we herein construct HKUST nMOFs as the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) self-supplying nanocarriers for efficient delivery of the DSF drug. The mildly acidic condition of tumor microenvironment initially triggered the release of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) ions from HKUST nMOFs, which further reacted with the encapsulated DSF to form toxic \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{DDTO})_{2}\) (activation) for tumor chemotherapy. Especially, during the \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{DDTO})_{2}\) complexation, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)species were formed concomitantly, triggering the intratumoral nanocatalytic therapy for the generation of reactive oxygen species to synergistically destroying the tumor cells/tissue. As a result, synergetic tumor-responsive chemotherapy and nanocatalytic therapy are enabled by DSF@HKUST nanodrugs, as demonstrated by the dominant anticancer efficacy with satisfied biocompatibility both in vitro and in vivo. The present work offers a sophisticated strategy for tumor-responsive nontoxic-to-toxic therapeutic with high biocompatibility.

# KEYWORDS

disulfiram, metal-organic frameworks, chemotherapy, Fenton-like reaction, tumor suppression

# 1 Introduction

Traditional chemotherapy has been clinically favorable treatment approach in cancer therapy for almost a century [1–3]. A variety of chemodrugs has been explored and developed as chemotherapeutic drugs for carcinoma destruction [4, 5]. In a large extent, chemotherapy has made a great step towards cancer healing. However, due to the intrinsic biological toxicities of most chemotherapeutic drugs, the non-tumor-site distribution into the human body is frequently associated with the intensified systemic side impacts, thereby limiting the ultimate therapeutic outcome against malignant tumors [6]. Targeting medications (e.g., Sorafenib) has been demonstrated to mitigate the undesired toxicities [7, 8], but their applications against tumors with such a variety are still challenging.

As the tumor cells progress and proliferate, these malignant tumor cells could adapt and develop several biological features that are distinct from the normal tissue cells. Typically, with the enhanced glycolysis process, the metabolic product of such a process lactate acid may eventually accumulate inside the tumor cells, constituting the mild acidic microenvironment with pH ca. 6.0 [9, 10]. In addition, with the exaggerated

As the tumor cells progress and proliferate, these malignant tumor cells could adapt and develop several biological features that are distinct from the normal tissue cells. Typically, with the enhanced glycolysis process, the metabolic product of such a process lactate acid may eventually accumulate inside the tumor cells, constituting the mild acidic microenvironment with pH ca. 6.0 [9, 10]. In addition, with the exaggerated single-electron reduction reaction of oxygen molecules towards superoxide anions radicals in mitochondria, tumor cells upregulate the superoxide dismutase (SOD) to eliminate these radicals, simultaneously leading to the production of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [11, 12]. With these two typical biological hallmarks, extensive tumor-responsive therapeutic modalities have been developed, including the acidity-responsive hydrogel drug delivery and nanocatalyst-based nanocatalytic therapy [13–15]. However, few reports have been focused on the design of tumor-responsive chemotherapeutics with the chemodrugs originating from other disease-corresponding therapeutic agents, which is of high significance because exploring new biomedical use of traditional drugs in different disease models can reduce the cost of drug development and overcome several critical issues of traditional chemodrugs such as low chemotherapeutic efficiency, severe side effect and drug resistance.

Disulfiram (DSF) is a US Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved pharmaceutical agent for the treatment of alcohol withdrawal and chronic alcoholism in the past decades [16, 17]. Recently, pilot studies revealed that DSF molecules were featured with broad-spectrum anti-cancer activity against varied types of tumors including multidrug resistance tumor
