were relatively stable at \(\mathrm{pH} = 7.4\) according to the slow-releasing profile of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) species (52.7% in \(48\,\mathrm{h}\)). In contrast, approximately \(93.1\%\) of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) rapidly released from HKUST nMOFs under mild acidic condition \(\mathrm{pH}\,6.0\) within the identical period, sufficiently validating that HKUST nMOFs could gradually degrade in the mild tumor microenvironment. For DSF counterpart, the drug-releasing profiles from HKUST nMOFs were determined as well by ICP-OES under different
pH conditions (Fig. 2(c)). It has been found that \(47.34\%\) of DSF could be released at neutral PBS within \(48\ \mathrm{h}\), while approximately \(82.48\%\) DSF was released under acidic environment within the identical duration. These results proved that both \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and DSF could be rapidly released from DSF@HKUST nanodrugs in response to the mildly acidic tumor microenvironment. Subsequent formation of the bis(dialkylammonium)-tetrathiolanedication \((\mathrm{Bitt}{-}4^{2+})\) leads to the simultaneous generation of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)species through the two-electron reduction process [39]. Subsequently, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)rapidly triggers DSF to form \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{DDTC})_{2}\) and catalyzes \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into highly toxic \(\bullet\mathrm{OH}\) [40].

To further explore the ROS production performance of DSF@HKUST nanodrugs, electron spin resonance (ESR) measurement was performed using 3,4-dihydro-2-methyl-1,1-dimethylethyl ester \(-2\mathrm{H}\)-pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid-1-oxide (BMPO) to trap the possible generated hydroxyl radicals. As shown from the ESR spectra (Fig. 2(d)), no observable radical signals were detected for HKUST nMOFs, even in the presence of high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) concentration \((5\,\mathrm{\mM})\). Comparatively, the supplementation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \((5\,\mathrm{\mM})\) to DSF@HKUST nanodrug dispersion yielded prominent \(\bullet\mathrm{OH}\) radical signals (characteristic peak intensity ratio of 12:2:1). Collectively for DSF@HKUST nanodrug within the mild acidity and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) conditions, synergetic \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{DDTC})_{2}\) formation and cascade \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)-mediated nanocatalytic radical generations were imparted, enabling the high potentials for carcinoma destruction both in vitro and in vivo.

To further evaluate \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)production in the presence of DSF, \(^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}\)-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) was utilized to detect \(\bullet\mathrm{OH}\) generation efficiency in the following assay groups: \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) only, \(\mathrm{DSF}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\mathrm{DSF}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Fig. S9(a) in the ESM). \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{DSF}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) groups showed lower activity after co-incubation with TMB for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\). Whereas, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) mixed with TMB exhibited moderate \(\bullet\mathrm{OH}\) generation efficiency. Compared to the other groups, it could be indicated

that the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\mathrm{DSF}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) assay group featured the highest chromogenic optical absorption at \(650\,\mathrm{nm}\), which was attributed to the hydroxyl radicals generated from the Fenton-like reaction in the presence of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\) (Fig. S9(b) in the ESM).

The intracellular therapeutic process is summarized in Fig. 3(a). The cytotoxicity profiles of HKUST nMOFs, HKUST nMOFs + \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), DSF and DSF@HKUST nanodrugs at varied concentrations were investigated by the typical cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8) method. Upon co-incubation with HKUST nMOFs or HKUST nMOFs + \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\), satisfactory cytocompatibility could be seen at respective concentration of as high as \(100\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (Figs. S10 and S11 in the ESM). 4-T1 cancer cells treated with DSF (0 to \(20\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) in the presence or absence of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) \((20\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) remained high viability within an identical time-period, manifesting the non-toxic behavior of both HKUST nMOFs and DSF drug (Fig. 3(b) and Fig. S12 in the ESM). Comparatively, when the cancer cells were co-incubated with DSF@HKUST nanodrug, the dose-dependent cytotoxicity profile could be monitored. The cell viabilities were only \(42.01\%\) and \(28.36\%\) of the control when the DSF@HKUST nanodrug dosage reached \(100\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) under neutral \((\mathrm{pH}=7.4)\) or acidic condition \((\mathrm{pH}=6.0)\), respectively (Fig. 3(c)). In addition, we demonstrated that acidic medium has no cytotoxic effect to cell viability (Fig. S13 in the ESM). The high therapeutic effect of the nanodrugs was specifically attributed to the acidity-enhanced DSF and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) species release upon the HKUST nMOFs degradation. These results proved that DSF@HKUST nanodrug could significantly inhibit the proliferation of 4-T1 cancer cells, as compared to either DSF or \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{DDTC})_{2}\) complexes [21]. As the supplementation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (as high as \(500\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\)) contributed negligible cytotoxicity against the 4-T1 tumor cells (Fig. S14 in the ESM), we then investigated th